<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Sudan</h1><p class="st-meta">1880 CE – 1960 CE · capital Bamako</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French colony on the upper and middle Niger, conquered by military campaigns from 1880, which became independent as Mali in 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French officers began the conquest of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> in 1880 as the military territory of the Upper River, pushing east from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>. Commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-borgnis-desbordes">Gustave Borgnis-Desbordes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gallieni">Joseph Gallieni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-archinard">Louis Archinard</a> built forts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kita-mali">Kita</a> in 1881 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> in 1883 and defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/toucouleur-empire">Toucouleur Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Toure</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wassoulou-empire">Wassoulou Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kenedougou-kingdom">Kenedougou</a> by 1898.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory changed name several times, becoming Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> in 1904 and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in 1920, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> from 1908. It was part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-niger-railway">Dakar-Niger railway</a> linked it to the coast, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/office-du-niger">Office du Niger</a>, founded in 1932, built irrigation works for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> using forced labour. Most of the population were Muslim farmers and herders.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibo-keita">Modibo Keita's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudanese-union-african-democratic-rally">Sudanese Union</a> led the territory to autonomy as the Sudanese Republic in 1958. It joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a> in 1959; after Senegal withdrew, it declared independence as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> on 22 September 1960.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-sudan.jpg" alt="French Sudan" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Akiramenai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Sudan_(1958%E2%80%931959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1880 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bamako</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joseph Gallieni, Louis Archinard, Modibo Keita</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Bambara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Upper Senegal and Niger, Soudan francais, Sudanese Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=french-sudan" aria-label="French Sudan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4461 1706 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Sudan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4781 1962L5054 728M4781 1962L4523 1888M4781 1962L4830 1933M4781 1962L4958 1973"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M